  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss and capped at the bowl's rim. It is steeper in x, so overshoot shows there first. Without noise, x swings grow once the learning rate is above 2/3 (about 0.67).",
    "Noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with standard deviation 1.5 × √((64 − b)/(63·b)) for batch size b. That is 1.5 at b = 1 and exactly zero at b = 64.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at the true length of a step in the bowl's units (scale 1). The orange arrow is −learning rate × noisy slope, the way the ball moves next. The grey arrow is −learning rate × true slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 10 (diverged). While playing, there is one update every 6 host ticks (5 per second at speed 1).",
    "Noise comes from fixed seed 7, so Reset replays the same sequence. 'New run, keep last path' moves on to the next noise values and keeps the finished path as the ghost. Settings changed mid-run apply from the next step on."
  ]
}